🚀 Common Git Mistakes & How to Fix Them – A Practical Guide for Developers & DevOps

If you’ve ever worked with Git, you know it’s both powerful and… unforgiving.
 One wrong command, and suddenly your repo looks like a battlefield of conflicts, detached HEADs, or force-push disasters.

Here are the most common Git mistakes, why they happen, and how to fix them 👇
🔴 Mistake 1: Committing to the Wrong Branch
Why it happens: Forgetting to switch branches before starting work.
 Fix:
git checkout correct-branch
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
git branch -D wrong-branch
✅ Best Practice: Always use git status before committing.

🔴 Mistake 2: Large or Wrong Files Committed
Why it happens: Accidentally adding logs, binaries, or secrets.
 Fix:
git reset HEAD~1       # undo last commit (soft reset)
git restore <file>     # unstage/remove file
For sensitive data: use git filter-repo or BFG Repo Cleaner.
✅ Best Practice: Use .gitignore religiously.

🔴 Mistake 3: Merge Conflicts Nightmare
Why it happens: Multiple people editing the same file/lines.
 Fix:
Open conflicted file → resolve manually.
Mark conflict as resolved:
git add <file>
git commit

✅ Trick: Use tools like VS Code Merge Editor or git mergetool to visualize conflicts.
🔴 Mistake 4: Detached HEAD State
Why it happens: Checking out a commit instead of a branch.
 Fix:
git checkout -b new-branch
✅ Tip: Always work on branches, not commits.

🔴 Mistake 5: Force Push Gone Wrong 😨
Why it happens: Using git push -f on shared branches.
 Fix:
If history was rewritten → restore with git reflog.
git reflog
git checkout <commit-hash>
git branch restore-branch
✅ Best Practice: Only force push on your feature branches, never on main/master.

🛠️ Pro Tips & Tricks
Use aliases for faster commands (git lg, git st).
Set up branch protection rules in GitHub/GitLab.
Practice commit hygiene: small, meaningful commits.
Before running risky commands, create a backup branch.
git checkout -b safe-copy

💡 Final Thought:
 Git mistakes are normal—even experienced engineers face them. What matters is knowing how to recover quickly and safely. With the right habits and best practices, you’ll turn Git from a “scary tool” into your best friend.
